  "response": "Geologists adjudicated between the impact and volcanic hypotheses not by argument from plausibility but by seeking signatures that one mechanism could produce and the other could not, and the dispute was accordingly framed as a test of two candidate causes: a hypervelocity bolide strike and a protracted episode of continental flood volcanism. The Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary is dated to approximately 66 million years ago. In 1980, Walter Alvarez and colleagues reported an anomalous enrichment of iridium in boundary clay at Gubbio, Italy. The same anomaly had been detected in Antarctic ice cores in 1972, eight years before the Italian measurements were published. Iridium is depleted in Earth's crust and relatively abundant in chondritic meteorites, and it is this disparity in abundance that shows the concentrations measured in the Gubbio clay to be anomalous rather than ordinary sedimentary background.\n\nMineralogy furnished a second diagnostic test, since shock metamorphism demands pressures that ordinary geological processes do not reach. Shocked quartz grains bearing multiple sets of deformation lamellae occur in Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary deposits. Basaltic flood eruptions routinely generate shocked quartz with planar deformation features; the multiply lamellated grains of the boundary beds, however, cannot be squared with such an eruptive origin, for these two accounts of how the grains were deformed cannot both be true. Glass spherules interpreted as impact-melt ejecta are preserved in boundary beds around the Gulf of Mexico and the Caribbean, and the Deccan Traps are flood basalts in western India emplaced near the end of the Cretaceous; the preservation of such melt droplets ringing the Gulf and the emplacement of that Indian province at the close of the Cretaceous cannot both be true. The existence of that Indian basalt province is itself what indicates that flood eruptions can shock quartz in this way.\n\nBecause flood basalt on that scale was erupted in western India, large volumes of sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide were released into the atmosphere. The chronological question then became the crux, because a volcanic kill mechanism requires that the disappearance of taxa track the eruptive history closely. Non-avian dinosaurs disappear from the fossil record at the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene boundary. Gerta Keller argued that the Chicxulub impact preceded the extinction horizon by about 300,000 years. Argon-argon dating by Renne and colleagues in 2013 placed the Chicxulub impact within about 32,000 years of the boundary. Even granting that loading of the atmosphere by volcanic gases, the radiometric age ties the crater to the extinction horizon within a few tens of thousands of years, which left timing rather than mechanism as the decisive battleground.\n\nThe last test was structural and was directed at the crater itself. Three years after that dating, in 2016, International Ocean Discovery Program Expedition 364 drilled the Chicxulub peak ring. The drilling resulted in a core that recovered shocked granitic basement rock overlain by impact melt and suevite. Taken together with the iridium anomaly, the shock mineralogy and the ejecta deposits, this stratigraphy left the impact mechanism as the only account compatible with the whole body of observations, with Deccan volcanism assigned a contributory rather than a primary role.",
